<title>USA: U.S. April personal income rose 0.1 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. April personal income rose 0.1 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-06-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Commerce Department personal income and spending estimates, in seasonally adjusted annual rates.</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  April    Mar--(Prev)   Feb--(Prev)   Jan</p>
<p> Income (Current Dlrs)  0.1    0.6    0.6    0.8    0.8    0.4</p>
<p>Disposable Income:</p>
<p> -- Current Dlrs	  0.1    0.5    0.6    0.7    0.8    0.2</p>
<p> -- Chain '92 Dlrs     Unch    0.5    0.5    0.4    0.5    0.1</p>
<p>Consumption:</p>
<p> -- Current Dlrs	  0.1    0.2    0.5    0.3    0.5    1.2</p>
<p> -- Chain '92 Dlrs     Unch    0.2    0.4   Unch    0.2    1.1</p>
<p>    Billions of Dlrs: April    Mar--(Prev)   Feb--(Prev)   Jan</p>
<p> Income		   6,753  6,746  6,745  6,707  6,706  6,653</p>
<p> Disposable Income    5,820  5,815  5,851  5,786  5,818  5,748</p>
<p> Personal Outlays     5,524  5,519  5,541  5,508  5,516  5,493</p>
<p> Pers.Consumption     5,355  5,350  5,371  5,338  5,346  5,324</p>
<p> -Durable Goods	   646    656    664    659    660    663</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods   1,595  1,599  1,607  1,595  1,601  1,591</p>
<p> -Services		3,114  3,095  3,100  3,084  3,085  3,070</p>
<p> Personal Savings	 295    296    310    278    302    256</p>
<p> Pct Savings Rate	 5.1    5.1    5.3    4.8    5.2    4.4</p>
<p>    Billions of Dlrs: April    Mar--(Prev)   Feb--(Prev)   Jan</p>
<p> Wages and Salaries   3,825  3,828  3,827  3,801  3,800  3,750</p>
<p> -Manufacturing	   700    699    699    692    693    689</p>
<p>-Service Industries   1,352  1,353  1,354  1,342  1,341  1,317</p>
<p> -Government		659    658    659    656    657    653</p>
<p> Proprietors' Income    552    551    552    549    550    545</p>
<p> -Farm			 45     45     45     46     45     46</p>
<p> -Non-Farm		  506    505    507    504    504    500</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected both personal income and spending for April to be unchanged from March.</p>
<p>April's 0.1 percent rise in personal income was the smallest increase since a 0.1 percent rise in October. The rise in spending was the smallest since a 0.1 percent rise in September, the department said.</p>
<p>The 1.6 percent decline in April durable goods spending is the steepest since a 2.9 percent drop in September 1996, the department said.</p>
